Host-owned window and loop: the runtime creates no window or audio device and the host drives the game thread

What it fixes: PS2Runtime::initialize() always opened a raylib window, an audio device and capped the
frame rate at 60, and run() was one monolithic function (window loop + game thread). A program that
embeds the runtime and does its own windowing, input, sound and presentation (the DON port: SDL3 and
the PCSX2 GS) could not do that. Adds, in ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h and src/lib/ps2_runtime.cpp:

  setHostOwnsWindow(bool) / hostOwnsWindow()   before initialize(): no SetConfigFlags, InitWindow,
        InitAudioDevice, SetTargetFPS, no debug UI init; the runtime's own sound playback is left
        "not ready" so it never reaches raylib.
  startGameThread()      the head of the old run() (reset of SIF/IOP/audio/MPEG stub state, kernel state,
        registers) plus the std::thread; returns at once. false if already started.
  isGameThreadRunning(), gameThreadFailed()   running until the EE scheduler has returned; failed if the
        game thread ended with an exception (it is printed as before).
  stopGameThread()       requestStop() and join; safe when not started and repeated. The destructor calls
        it (a joinable std::thread must not be destroyed).
  run()                  unchanged for the runtime's own executable (it is now startGameThread() + the same
        raylib loop + stopGameThread()). With setHostOwnsWindow(true) it only waits, without drawing, for
        the game thread to end or requestStop().

raylib call sites and what happens to them with the host owning the window (none is reached, on any thread):
  initialize(): SetConfigFlags, InitWindow, InitAudioDevice, IsAudioDeviceReady, SetTargetFPS -> skipped;
  run(): GenImageColor, LoadTextureFromImage, UnloadImage, UploadFrame (UpdateTexture), BeginDrawing,
        ClearBackground, GetScreenWidth/Height, DrawTexturePro, EndDrawing, WindowShouldClose,
        UnloadTexture, CloseWindow -> only in the branch of a runtime that owns its window;
  ~PS2Runtime(): IsAudioDeviceReady, CloseAudioDevice, IsWindowReady, CloseWindow -> guarded by the flag;
  ps2_audio.cpp (Load/Play/Stop/Unload Sound, IsSoundPlaying): only reached through playDecodedSample,
        which returns first while the audio is "not ready" (always the case here); stopAll/prune iterate
        an empty list;
  the pad reads (ps2_pad.cpp, Kernel/Stubs/Pad.cpp: IsKeyDown, IsGamepad*, GetGamepadAxisMovement) run on
        the game thread and are handled by patch 0008.
A fix inside startGameThread(): EeScheduler::reset() clears the scheduler's stop flag, so a stop
requested between start and that reset (a host calling stopGameThread() right after startGameThread())
was lost and the join hung; the thread now re-applies a pending stop after reset(). The old run() had the
same window, only much narrower.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/host_loop_test.cpp, with DISPLAY unset: a PS2Runtime with host
functions registered as the guest and a fake host GS; 131 vsyncs in 2.2 s (60.00 Hz), thread_begin,
vsyncs, thread_end in order and on one thread that is the guest's thread and not the caller's; start and
stop return; stop right after start 20 times; run() of a host-owned runtime returns on requestStop();
every raylib function the library references is replaced in the test by a recorder: 0 calls with the
host owning the window, and a runtime that does not own it records SetConfigFlags, InitWindow,
InitAudioDevice, SetTargetFPS (positive control). Not exercised: the runtime's own raylib loop (needs a
display) and the game's generated code.

Based on: original work for this project against PS2Recomp commit
2c5fbb9389e11dd95693385969490c9e8e6f57b4, on top of patches 0001 to 0006.

